n of a changed general-election environment"], "rationale": "California's durable 12-point Democratic lean is the only substantive evidence available, since no general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures exist yet. With no evidence pointing away from the fundamentals, the forecast holds at a double-digit Democratic edge with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Any credible statewide general-election polling or expert ratings that emerge as the 2028 field settles could shift the margin, especially if national conditions move sharply toward either party.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "California remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in presidential politics, and the baseline for the 2028 open-seat race is a Democratic margin of roughly 12 points. No incumbent is on the ballot, and neither party has settled on a nominee, with headlines focused on a crowded Democratic primary featuring figures like Newsom, Harris, and Ossoff, and a clearer early picture on the Republican side.\n\nThere are no general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising data to refine the picture, so the forecast rests entirely on the state's partisan fundamentals. That supports a Likely Democratic rating with relatively wide uncertainty, since primary outcomes and national conditions could still move the race, though a Republican win would require an unusually large shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9157, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8615, "margin": 12.0}]}